The worst row in the audit, because the workaround is more dangerous than the
gap. edit writes a body three ways: an editor, which an agent cannot drive;
-a, which appends; and -b, which replaces the whole thing. So changing one
paragraph non-interactively means reconstructing the entire body and piping
it back, and anything left out is silently deleted.

That is how 24 and 28 were corrected in 99ebca6 — extract with awk, replace a
passage in a temp file, pipe it all back — and it survived review only
because the tickets were short enough to check by eye. The tickets in this
tracker are no longer short.

The failure has no signal. A truncated body is a valid ticket: check has
nothing to say about it, the file parses, and the loss shows up whenever
someone next reads the ticket and the paragraph they remember is gone. Every
other manual idiom in this audit fails loudly.

What would remove it, roughly in order of how much they assume:

- A way to replace a passage by match rather than by rewriting the file.
- A way to edit in place through a filter, so the caller transforms what is
  there instead of reconstructing it.
- Accepting the risk and making the reconstruction safe: have -b refuse a
  body dramatically shorter than the one it replaces unless forced. Cheap,
  and catches the actual failure rather than preventing it.

The last one is worth taking seriously even if a better command lands, since
it costs little and the failure it catches is the one that has already
happened twice.

Done when changing a paragraph does not require reconstructing the body, or
the reconstruction cannot silently lose one, and the suite covers a body that
would have been truncated.


Done 2026-09-18. Both halves, as decided: the guard and the passage replace.

  pidly edit -r "the old sentence." -w "the new one." 7
  pidly edit -b 7 < body.md        refuses a body that lost most of itself
  pidly edit -f -b 7 < body.md     unless you say you meant it

-r matches literally, not as a regex, and the text must appear exactly once.
Literal because a body quotes shell, awk and patterns constantly — this
tracker's own tickets do it on nearly every line — and a caller made to escape
what they can see in front of them will get it wrong. Exactly once because a
passage appearing twice is a caller who means one of them and cannot say
which; replacing both, or the first, is a guess the command has no business
making. Absent and ambiguous are both refused with a message that says which.

The guard: -b refuses a replacement under half the size of a body of at least
200 bytes. The numbers are arbitrary and the reference says so rather than
pretending they were tuned — the point is to catch a reconstruction that
dropped most of the file, not to police editing. -f is the escape hatch, and
append is never guarded because appending cannot lose anything.

One bug worth recording, caught in testing rather than by reasoning: the
first version of replace_passage read the whole info file, so the header was
folded into the body and written back as prose. The fix is the same boundary
get_body uses — the first blank line — and there is now a test asserting line
3 is still the title after a replace.

Both strings travel through the environment rather than awk -v, for the
reason 48 found: awk -v processes escape sequences. That is also why -r can
be given text containing \t or a backslash and have it match what is in the
file.

Covered in edit.sh: replacement, the surrounding paragraphs surviving, the
header surviving, absent and duplicate passages, -w without -r, a literal
regex-looking passage, the guard refusing and leaving the body untouched, -f,
a small body not being policed, and append never being guarded.
